The spline camera updates by first increasing the t value as a function of speed and delta time.
If the t value is greater than or equal to 1.0, P
1
advances to the next point on the path (assuming
that there are enough points on the path). Advancing P
1
also means you must subtract 1 from
the t value. If the spline has no more points, the spline camera pauses.
For the camera calculations, the position of the camera is simply the point computed
from the spline. To compute the target point, you increase t by a small delta to determine
the direction the spline camera is moving. Finally, the up vector stays at (0, 0, 1), which
assumes that you do not want the spline to flip upside down. Listing 9.8 gives the code for
SplineCamera::Update, and Figure 9.7 shows the spline camera in action.
